# [#2906] Build context for docker/local-control-plane/Dockerfile.
#
# `problems/` and `.gitmodules` are deliberately excluded, not merely large:
# `problems/` is bind-mounted read-only at runtime (see docker-launcher.sh) so
# the container always serves whatever the participant's own clone actually
# has, and excluding `.gitmodules` makes `autoInitProblemsSubmodule()`
# (scripts/local-play/catalog-loader.ts) a clean no-op inside the image instead
# of shelling out to a `git` that has no `.git` history to work with.
.git
.gitmodules
problems/

node_modules
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/coverage
**/cdk.out
**/.turbo
.tenkacloud/

# この image が作るのは control plane と participant portal だけ。 workspace ですら無い
# (= `bun install` がその package.json を読まない) 木を context に載せる理由が無い。
# 実測: tmp/ 389MB、 sampleimages/ 33MB、 docs/ 8.3MB、 landing/ 5.0MB。
# `infrastructure` と `apps/*` は workspace なので同じ扱いにはできない — `bun install
# --frozen-lockfile` がそれぞれの package.json を要求する。 中身の重い cdk.out は上で除外済み。
tmp/
sampleimages/
docs/
landing/

# [#2906 audit finding] A host/dev session (`make local-dev`) writes this file
# with that session's live participant token — `COPY . .` would otherwise bake
# a developer's leftover session credential into the image. The container
# path never reads it (server.ts serves /runtime-config.json dynamically), so
# excluding it is pure hygiene, not a functional requirement.
apps/participant-portal/public/runtime-config.json
apps/participant-portal/public/runtime-config.backup.json

# [#2906 round-2 audit finding] .gitignore ignores .env / .env.* (a developer's
# working tree may hold real secrets there — Turso tokens etc., AGENTS.md
# treats these as sensitive) but that alone does not stop `COPY . .` from
# baking them into this image; only .dockerignore does. The image never runs
# outside this machine (tenkacloud-local:dev, no push/registry path in this
# repo), but `docker save`/layer inspection would otherwise leak them, and
# this file already applies exactly this standard to runtime-config.json above.
#
# `**/.env` is NOT redundant with `.env`, and dropping it silently reopens the
# hole this section exists to close. .dockerignore patterns are anchored at the
# context root (they are NOT .gitignore semantics), so a bare `.env` matches the
# ROOT .env and nothing else — a workspace-level `infrastructure/.env` or
# `apps/*/.env` still lands in the image. Verified empirically against Docker
# Engine 29.3.1 with `docker build --output type=local` on a fixture tree: with
# `.env` alone, `sub/.env`, `sub/deep/.env`, and `apps/portal/.env` were all
# still present in the build result; adding `**/.env` excluded every depth while
# `!**/.env.example` still kept the examples at every depth.
.env
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# NOTE: every `workspaces` member (package.json: infrastructure, apps/*,
# packages/*) is deliberately kept in the build context even though this
# image only RUNS scripts/ and apps/participant-portal — `bun install
# --frozen-lockfile` resolves the lockfile against the full workspace graph,
# and excluding a member's source (as opposed to its build artifacts above)
# is unverified in this environment (no live `docker build` access here, see
# the PR's Known limitations) and risks a partial-workspace install that
# silently resolves differently from every other `bun install` in this repo.
